nv91-asa@nada.kth.se (Anders Sandberg) wrote:

>Curt Adams wrote:
>>Giving a human a more sophisticated color system - like the bird 4-color
>>system rather than the primate 3-color - would be much more difficult.  The
>>optic nerve and optic cortex are only geared up to carry and analyze three
>>different color signals.  Adding a fourth would require advances in our
>>knowledge on development biology as we do not yet know much about the
genetic
>>controls for that.  I would suspect that changes would require multiple
>>genetic changes (unlike the single change for the rhodopsin switch).

>What would happen if we added the genes for a 4-color system anyway? As
>far as I know, the visual system seems to self-organize to a large degree
>during development, and might quite possibly develop to use 4-color
>instead. As an example of how flexible it can be, researchers have
>implanted a third eye precursor during embryonal growth of frogs, and they
>then develop into three eyed frogs which apparently can see using the
>third eye (I don't know how well they see or how good control they have of
>the movements of the third eye, but it is linked to their visual cortex). 

Well, we don't know.  We don't know what the genes are, or even how many
there are.  Embryological genes often have broad effects - one change may
affect many systems at once.  I didn't know that the three-eyed frogs could
see,  so things may not be quite as tough as I thought.  

Rhodopsin is basically controlled by only one gene.  Features of
embryological development, however, emerge from large numbers of genes
interacting.  Kaufman's "Origins of Order" (GREAT book) discusses how
randomnly interconnected genes can produce different cell lines. 

In this model, most genes merely control other genes, turning them on and
off.  Only a few genes actually do "real work" but all the other genes serve
as control and coordination for those few.  The problem, assuming his model
is basically correct, is that differentiation involves holistic behavior of
all controlling genes, which would be about 100,000 for humans.  The vast
number, incidentally, serves not as a minimum necessary for the control, but
as a minimum for the appropriate level of control to emerge spontaneously
from a random system.  As one changes genes, one by one, the system usually
does not change or changes slightly.  Every now and then, though, one change
causes drastic, and unpredictable, effects.  

Given the 300 million years or so separating birds and humans, the bird
systems and human systems have diverged substantially.  Hence the bird genes
will have essentially random effects on human biology (because the genes THEY
control have no clear analogue in the human system).  Certainly we will need
detailed information on these interactions, in both birds and human before we
can even try the switch; we don't yet even know all the genes in humans, much
less your target bird species.


