From: CurtAdams@aol.com
Date: Fri, 9 Dec 1994 20:27:27 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-399] SING: Are we still evolving?

vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner) says

>It is interesting to note that there is still quite a bit of "room" for
>human evolution, genetically speaking.  There are 4 different DNA bases
>used to encode genetic sequences, and these bases are used in groups of 3
>to encode each amino acid in a protein.  This gives a possible 4 x 4 x 4,
>or 64, combinations for different amino acid sequences.  However, proteins
>are built from a set of only 20 basic amino acids.  This gives a genetic
>"headroom" of 44 / 20, or 220%, potentially new amino acids for use in
>protein construction.  I have no idea what these new compounds would be, >or
how they would work, but I am confident that they will eventually occur,
>given time.

After at least 4 billion years of separate evolution (if we even have the
same ancestor), we and bacteria still have the same codon map.  Mutations
tend to kill individuals by changing so many proteins at once, and to
exterminate species by cutting them off from swapping genes with all the
other critters about.  Any naturally evolved descendants of us will almost
certainly have the same codon map.

Of course, we could change the map artificially.  A complete change in the
map, with associated changes to transcribed proteins and auxiliary item like
transfer RNA, would create a nearly identical creature with a completely
different DNA.  Said creature would have one very substantial advantage -
current viruses could not affect it.  It also could not breed or swap genes
with any existing earth species.

