>Curt Adams says
>
>reptilian = brainstem
>mammalian = limbic system
>3rd layer = cerebral cortex
>
>The cortex does complex processing, but does not create conciousness, at
>least not by itself.

This must be the theory presented in "The Triune Brain in Evolution" by
Paul MacLean, Plenum, New York, 1990.   This looks to me like a sort of
baby-theory: the brain has hundreds of structures, not three, each with a
long evolutionary history that is linked to several other brain organs--but
not all of them.  So the cerebral structures do not form three clear layes,
nor even layers at all.  What we see is a netowrk of hundreds of brain
organs, whose structure shows only a confused remnant of their evolutionary
chronology.  Some "early" brain organs have surely changed their functions
a little, to accomodate new demands from later ones, and so on.  One should
read theories like MacLean's the way you'd read a science fiction novel,
hoping  to get, say some good ideas from it, but one should not expect an
so simple model to be very good.

There's a review of MacLean's book by Anton Reiner in Science, 12 October
1990.  It cites many examples of evidence against MacLean's speculations.
The review concludes:--

>One could also say that the book, both in its general tone and through the
>allusions to and quotations from the classics and philosophical works, clearly
>reveals MacLean to be a scholarly man with great interests in philosophy and
>the welfare of mankind. Nonetheless, there are some telling shortcomings in
>his scholarship. For example, in his presentation on the evolution of the
>R-complex, MacLean makes a comment that should leave Stephen I. Gould, not to
>mention all other students of evolution, aghast. Claiming that the concept of
>homology is confused and not clearly defined, he discards it in favor of what
>he regards as a much less equivocal term, namely correspondence. This is a
>very critical misjudgment to make in a work on evolution. MacLean also errs in
>his apparent sweeping acceptance of Haeckel's idea that ontogeny recapitulates
>phylogeny.

