The problem I have with your procedure is that is makes one's current and 
particular desires all-important. I espouse an ethics of virtue (as 
distinguished from a consequentialist or deontological morality). On an 
ethics of virtue, character is primary rather than actions, outcomes, or
abstract rules. The virtuous person is one who lives in accordance with their
nature. Living virtuously means _flourishing_. The basic moral facts are
those about what is constitutively and instrumentally needed for life as the
kind of being we are, that is, for a flourishing life. (Virtue approaches to
ethics have long been unpopular, until very recently, though Aristotle and
Nietzsche can be regarded as holding virtue-based views.) 

I am not a relativist because I contend that, as human beings, many common
conditions apply to us in determining what makes a flourishing life. The 
conditions for living well largely are not open to our choice. (If we change
our basic biological and psychological nature in the future, some of the
conditions for our flourishing may change.) On the other hand, I would not
argue that ethical behavior is in any way compulsory. If someone simply
has no interest in flourishing, there's not much you can say to them (at 
leat if they persist in their lack of interest after explaining what it is
to flourish). 

Attempting to live virtuously, rather than deciding what's right for you on
the basis of your transitory desires, helps form an integrated, strong core
of personality. Virtues, principles, and the projects and life-plans they 
inform, strengthen us, deepen our sense of self, and help us resist being
pushed around by the vagaries of external forces. "What kind of person do I
want to be?" I see as properly prior to the question "what do I most want to
do or gain in this situation?" As a pragmatic aspect of a virtue approach,
I claim that a concern for character and virtue over pursuit of your wants,
will be more likely to support self-esteem, to foster healthy relationships,
and to earn the good regard of others.

On this List and elsewhere, often noted is the decline in personal 
responsibility we see in the general population. People sue one another
to gain a quick profit, they demand benefits from the State while whining
when it comes time to pay, and they fail to take care of their physical,
intellectual, and psychological health. I believe that part of the cause of 
this is the subjectivist and relativist line pushed in schools (even made 
into educational theories such as "progressivism") and through much of the
media. Whim, desire, and greed have been replacing the character and virtue
that create and sustain strong, autonomous selves. To borrow a line, the 
world is perishing from an orgy of self-sacrificing.

Virtue ethics, with its basis in an idea of flourishing, or the good as health,
is an ethics of self-realization. As such, it seems highly extropic.

